{"id":9,"course_id":3,"title":"Docker Images, Tags, Digests, and Registries","summary":"Learn how application artifacts move from laptop to cluster.","sequence":1,"body":"## Artifact path\nMost Kubernetes releases start with an image pushed to a registry. Tags are human-friendly labels. Digests are content addresses. Production systems should know exactly which image content they are running.\n- A tag like `1.4.2` can be moved unless your registry prevents it.\n- A digest identifies immutable content.\n- Registries need authentication, retention, scanning, and promotion rules.\n- Use multi-stage builds to reduce runtime image size and attack surface.\n\n## Local-safe commands\n$ docker build -t checkout:local .\n$ docker image inspect checkout:local\n$ docker tag checkout:local registry.example.com/checkout:1.4.2\n$ docker image ls\nDo not push to a real registry unless you own it. For learning, inspect Dockerfiles and image metadata locally.\n\n## Production tradeoff\nFloating tags make demos fast but make incident review painful. Digests make rollout evidence stronger.","practice_notes":"Scenario: Production and staging both say they run `latest`, but behavior differs.\nChecklist:\n- Compare image digests, not only tags.\n- Check who can retag or overwrite images.\n- Confirm the deployment record stores the digest or immutable version.\n- Decide whether promotion should copy images or promote references.\nCommands:\n$ docker image inspect checkout:local --format '{{json .RepoDigests}}'","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-cloud-native-foundations","course_category":"Cloud Native","course_era":"Platform Academy","terms":[{"id":41,"term":"Registry","context":"Artifact store","definition":"A service that stores and serves container images."},{"id":42,"term":"Tag","context":"Image label","definition":"A human-friendly image reference that can point to different content over time."},{"id":43,"term":"Digest","context":"Immutable reference","definition":"A content-addressed image identifier such as sha256."},{"id":44,"term":"Multi-stage build","context":"Dockerfile pattern","definition":"A build pattern that separates build tooling from the final runtime image."},{"id":45,"term":"Artifact promotion","context":"Release flow","definition":"Moving a reviewed artifact through environments without rebuilding it."}],"flashcards":[{"id":25,"lesson_id":9,"prompt":"Why are digests stronger evidence than tags?","answer":"A digest identifies exact content; a tag can be moved unless protected.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":26,"lesson_id":9,"prompt":"What is a registry?","answer":"A service that stores and serves container images.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":27,"lesson_id":9,"prompt":"Why use multi-stage builds?","answer":"To keep build tools out of the runtime image and reduce size and attack surface.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
